Keep an rpc2 server connection alive until its in-flight requests complete.

A server request can still be in the VFS when its connection disconnects, and
the reply it eventually produces must not run against a freed connection or a
recycled bind.  A server request now takes a reference on its connection at
dispatch (request->conn_pinned) and releases it at the single request-free
chokepoint, so the connection object outlives every request that may still
reply on it.  On disconnect the connection is latched closed up front and the
handler holds a guard reference across its own body, so any reference a notify
callback or the client pending_calls completion drops cannot free the
connection mid-handler; its teardown (reassembly iovecs, RPCSEC_GSS contexts,
free) runs exactly once, when the guard and every in-flight server reference
are gone.  The reply path drops a reply on a closed connection at the rpc2
layer -- before reading connection state and before touching the bind --
releasing the marshalled payload so nothing leaks; the bind, which may already
be recycled, is never dereferenced.  Client requests take no reference and are
unaffected.

A loopback test defers a server reply, disconnects the client, and then
completes the reply: it fails under AddressSanitizer against the old behaviour
(a read of the freed connection) and passes once the reference keeps the
connection alive and the late reply is dropped.

